        <!-- 2. HOW TO CALCULATE PLASTER QUANTITY -->
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">How to Calculate Plaster Quantity</h2>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Accurate estimation of wall plaster quantity follows standard civil engineering principles:
          </p>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            First calculate the total plaster area by multiplying wall length by wall height and multiplying by the number of walls. Then deduct doors, windows, and other openings to arrive at the <strong>Net Plaster Area</strong>. Multiply the net area by the specified plaster thickness (e.g. 12mm or 15mm) to estimate wet mortar volume.
          </p>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Because dry sand has internal void spaces that shrink when mixed with water and cement, apply the selected dry volume assumption (a dry factor of 1.30 to 1.33). Then add a wastage percentage (10% to 15%) and divide the dry mortar quantity according to the selected cement-to-sand ratio.
          </p>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Actual material requirements can vary because of surface condition, uneven masonry walls, workmanship, mortar thickness variations, material wastage, and mix design.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- 3. UNDERSTANDING CEMENT AND SAND MORTAR RATIOS -->
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Understanding Cement and Sand Mortar Ratios</h2>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            The proportions of cement to sand dictate compressive strength, bond adhesion, and water resistance:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>1:3 Mix:</strong> 1 part cement and 3 parts sand. A rich, high-strength mix used for RCC ceiling plaster, water retaining structures, damp-proof courses (DPC), and structural repairs.</li>
            <li><strong>1:4 Mix:</strong> 1 part cement and 4 parts sand. The international benchmark for standard internal wall plastering and single-brick masonry joints.</li>
            <li><strong>1:5 Mix:</strong> 1 part cement and 5 parts sand. A balanced, cost-effective mix suitable for general internal plastering on flat blockwork.</li>
            <li><strong>1:6 Mix:</strong> 1 part cement and 6 parts sand. An economical lean mix commonly specified for thick internal plaster coats and non-load-bearing partitions.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            The correct ratio depends on the application and project specifications. Always verify with local building codes before ordering materials.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- 4. TILE MORTAR CALCULATOR -->
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Tile Mortar Calculator</h2>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Our specialized <strong>tile mortar calculator</strong> helps contractors, tilers, and homeowners estimate dry adhesive and cementitious mortar bed requirements based on total floor or wall area, tile type (ceramic, porcelain, vitrified), adhesive coverage rates, bed thickness, bag size, and wastage allowances.
          </p>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Adhesive consumption varies by notched trowel profile: standard 6mm square notches consume roughly 4.0 to 4.5 kg/m², while 10mm to 12mm notches for large format vitrified tiles consume 5.5 to 7.0 kg/m². Manufacturer coverage specifications should always be checked for accurate product-specific procurement.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- 5. GAMING MORTAR CALCULATORS -->
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Gaming Mortar Calculator</h2>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            The gaming artillery section provides tactical simulation enthusiasts with precise 2D Euclidean distance and azimuth calculations derived from map grid coordinates (Delta X = X2 − X1, Delta Y = Y2 − Y1, Distance = sqrt((X2 − X1)² + (Y2 − Y1)²)).
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-4">Arma Reforger Mortar Calculator</h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            The <strong>arma reforger mortar calculator</strong> tool computes direct range and magnetic azimuth in standard 6400 NATO milliradians, enabling rapid battery deployment across Everon and Arland conflict zones.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-4">Arma Mortar Calculator</h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Using the <strong>arma mortar calculator</strong> mode, players operating the Mk6 82mm mortar can verify target distances and select optimal propellant charges (Charge 0, 1, or 2) for high-angle indirect fire missions.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-4">Squad Mortar Calculator</h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            The <strong>squad mortar calculator</strong> calculates target distance and converts meters into tube elevation mils using the standard 81mm/82mm ballistic reference curve (ranging between 50m and 1250m), accounting for positive or negative target elevation differentials.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- 6. PRACTICAL EXAMPLE -->
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Plaster Quantity Calculation Example</h2>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Consider a typical residential room wall requiring internal smooth plaster:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 space-y-1">
            <li><strong>Wall Length:</strong> 10.0 m</li>
            <li><strong>Wall Height:</strong> 3.0 m</li>
            <li><strong>Gross Wall Area:</strong> 10 × 3 = 30 m²</li>
            <li><strong>Openings Deduction:</strong> 0 m²</li>
            <li><strong>Plaster Thickness:</strong> 15 mm (0.015 m)</li>
            <li><strong>Mortar Ratio:</strong> 1:4 (1 part cement : 4 parts sand)</li>
            <li><strong>Dry Volume Factor:</strong> 1.30</li>
            <li><strong>Wastage Allowance:</strong> 10%</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-blue-200 bg-blue-50/50 p-4 dark:border-blue-900/50 dark:bg-blue-950/20 text-xs font-mono space-y-1 mt-3">
            <div>• Net Plaster Area = 30.00 m² (322.92 sq.ft)</div>
            <div>• Wet Mortar Volume = 30.00 × 0.015 = 0.450 m³</div>
            <div>• Dry Mortar Volume = 0.450 × 1.30 × 1.10 = 0.644 m³ (22.73 CFT)</div>
            <div>• Cement Required = (0.644 × 1/5) × 1440 = <strong>185.3 kg (3.71 Bags of 50kg)</strong></div>
            <div>• Sand Required = (0.644 × 4/5) = <strong>0.515 m³ = 18.18 CFT (0.82 Tonnes)</strong></div>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 mt-2 italic">
            This example is for estimation purposes. Actual material requirements may vary.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- 7. COMMON MISTAKES -->
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Common Mistakes When Calculating Plaster and Mortar</h2>
          
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-3">Forgetting Openings</h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Failing to deduct large patio doors, French windows, and ventilation openings results in significant over-ordering of cement and sand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-3">Using the Wrong Thickness</h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Specifying 12mm on unlevel brickwork where 18mm to 20mm is needed creates immediate material shortages on the job site.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-3">Ignoring Wastage</h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Mortar dropping onto scaffolding boards, rebound loss during throwing, and mixer residues routinely account for 8% to 15% material loss.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-3">Using the Wrong Mix Ratio</h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Applying an excessively rich 1:3 mix on internal walls causes shrinkage cracks, whereas a weak 1:6 mix on exposed external walls leads to damp penetration.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-3">Mixing Unit Systems</h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Combining wall measurements in feet with thickness in millimeters without proper conversion produces severe mathematical errors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-3">Assuming Tile Adhesive Coverage Is Always the Same</h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Adhesive coverage fluctuates dramatically between small ceramic subway tiles and large vitrified floor tiles requiring deep notch trowels.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- 8. IMPORTANT INFORMATION -->
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Important Information</h2>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 space-y-1.5 leading-relaxed">
            <li>Results provided by this calculator are engineering estimates based on entered theoretical parameters.</li>
            <li>Actual material requirements may vary due to surface conditions, joint thickness, sand moisture bulking, and site workmanship.</li>
            <li>Construction specifications should always follow approved architectural drawings and structural project requirements.</li>
            <li>Mortar ratios should be selected according to the intended application and environmental exposure.</li>
            <li>Tile adhesive coverage can vary by product, substrate porosity, and manufacturer instructions.</li>
            <li>Critical engineering or structural decisions should be verified by a qualified professional civil engineer.</li>
            <li>Gaming mortar calculations are reference coordinate tools and may not perfectly match every in-game server patch or weather modification.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

    </div>
  </Fragment>

  <!-- ========================================================= -->
  <!-- FAQ SECTION (EXACTLY 7 FAQS) -->
  <!-- ========================================================= -->
  <Fragment slot="faq">
    <div class="space-y-4 text-xs sm:text-sm">
      {faqs.map((faq, index) => (
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800" open={index === 0}>
          <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
            <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
              {index + 1}. {faq.q}
              <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
            </h3>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            {faq.a}
          </p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>

